.TH WOWUP-CF-INSTALLER 1 "September 2026" "wowup-cf-installer 0.3.0"
.SH NAME
wowup-cf-installer \- verified official WoWUp CurseForge application delivery
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wowup-cf-installer install --assumeyes
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer update --assumeyes
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer release --json
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer prepare --directory
.I DIR
.RB [ --app-version " VERSION"]
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer apply --appimage
.I PATH
.B --sha256
.I HEX
.B --app-version
.I VERSION
.B --assumeyes
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer status --json
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer uninstall --assumeyes
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer purge --assumeyes
.br
.B wowup-cf-installer --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
This helper targets Fedora 44 x86_64. The helper RPM contains no application
payload. Installing, reinstalling or upgrading the RPM automatically queues a
one-shot systemd job that downloads and installs the package-selected WoWUp
release. No second installer command is required. DNF completion reports queuing,
not application readiness. Check systemctl status wowup-cf-installer.service
and journalctl -u wowup-cf-installer.service for completion or failures.
The job waits for the DNF transaction and retries failed operations at
60-second intervals, limited to three starts in 15 minutes. Reinstalling the
helper queues a fresh attempt after a persistent problem is fixed.
Application updates arrive through new helper RPM release selections.
Final DNF package removal stops the job and its retries, then removes the
verified application bundle and desktop integration. Upgrades and reinstalls
do not uninstall the app. A stop, ownership or cleanup failure prevents erasing
the helper, allowing a later retry. Settings, games and addons are retained.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install, update
Require root and --assumeyes. Reconcile the release selected by the signed
helper package. Verify existing ownership before network access. A current,
verified installation is unchanged; missing known links can be repaired
offline. Refuse downgrades, changed release metadata and conflicting ownership.
Download the exact official AppImage and apply it using the same verification
and ownership rules as prepare/apply. Print installed-state JSON.
.TP
.B release
Print the package-selected version, source and SHA-256 as JSON, offline.
.TP
.B prepare
Run without root. Download the latest stable official CurseForge AppImage, or
the version selected with --app-version, into a private child of an existing
directory. Verify its exact HTTPS release URL, GitHub SHA-256, expected size and
x86_64 type-2 AppImage identity. Print metadata JSON with schema_version, name,
arch, version, sha256, path and source. The caller owns successful-download
cleanup and must show the selected artifact before approval. Failure removes
the incomplete download. These checks are not a publisher signature.
.TP
.B apply
Require root and --assumeyes. Work offline. Copy the selected artifact into
private privileged staging, verify it again, refuse downgrades and extract
with native unsquashfs without executing the downloaded runtime. Verify owned
files before replacement. Publish the bundle and install the fixed integration
links. Print verified installed-state JSON. The caller must approve the exact
version and SHA-256 before invoking this command.
.TP
.B status
Read-only and offline. Print JSON containing schema_version, name, installed,
verified, integrated and cleanup_pending, plus the approved identity when
installed. Invalid or foreign state is an error, never successful absence.
Missing known integration links are repairable through approved apply.
.TP
.B uninstall
Require root and --assumeyes. Verify ownership before removing integration
links and the application bundle. A durable removal journal permits resuming
interrupted removal. Foreign or modified surviving entries block cleanup.
Preserve home directories, application settings, games and addons.
.TP
.B purge
Run as your normal user, without sudo, with --assumeyes. Close WoWUp first.
Delete only the invoking user's $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/WowUpCf profile (default
~/.config/WowUpCf), including preferences, sessions, cookies, logs and caches.
Run before removing the helper RPM, while the command is available.
Leave the shared application, other users' profiles and game/addon files outside
this profile untouched. This command may also be used alone to reset WoWUp.
Reject root execution, a symlink profile, foreign ownership, special files,
crossings to another filesystem, and live or unrecognised Electron session locks.
Remove nested symlinks without following their targets. Do not use SUDO_USER.
Keep WoWUp closed during cleanup. Interrupted or failed cleanup may be partial;
fix the reported problem and rerun. An absent profile succeeds without changes.
Print JSON containing schema_version, name, path and purged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /opt/wowup-cf/
Owned AppImage, extracted app, launcher, desktop entry and inventory receipt.
.TP
.B /usr/local/bin/wowup-cf
Owned symlink to the launch wrapper.
.TP
.B /usr/local/share/applications/wowup-cf.desktop
Owned symlink to the desktop entry.
.TP
.B /opt/.nimbus-wowup-cf-removal.json
Durable ownership journal for interrupted removal.
.TP
.B /opt/.nimbus-wowup-cf-removing
Owned pending-removal bundle. These established paths retain their original
Nimbus names for lifecycle compatibility.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/WowUpCf
Current user's profile, defaulting to ~/.config/WowUpCf. Removed only by an
explicit unprivileged purge, never by DNF removal or uninstall.
.SH NOTES
The launcher clears AppImage updater environment variables, preserving addon
updates and Chromium sandboxing. The one-shot job runs on package changes,
not periodically or at every boot. Bundle exchange is atomic; the complete install or removal transaction
is not. Missing known links and interrupted removal can be retried, while
unknown ownership or modified files require inspection. Empty parent directories
remain after removal. DNF removal invokes the verified uninstall operation
automatically; standalone uninstall remains available for app-only removal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. Nonzero indicates invalid input, unsupported platform,
unverified ownership or a failed operation. SIGTERM and keyboard interruption
return nonzero. A failed operation must not receive a successful caller receipt.
